  "dom_id": "step-q216495",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $P(n):$ $n$ is odd or $n+1$ is odd\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$",
  "worked_solution": "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$n$ is odd\" is the set of odd natural numbers, and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+1$ is odd\" is the set of even natural numbers.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={\\text{odd natural numbers}}\\cup {\\text{even natural numbers}}=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
